HOUSE PRICE PREDICTION USING MACHINE LEARNING 
Organization: Maincrafts Technology 
Task: Artificial Intelligence & Machine Learning – Task 2 
Project: Feature Engineering, Model Optimization & Performance Comparison 
1. Objective 
The objective of this project is to develop a machine learning system that predicts 
median house values using the California Housing dataset. The project demonstrates 
data exploration, feature scaling, regression model training, performance evaluation, 
and best-model selection. 
2. Dataset and Technologies 
The California Housing dataset was obtained from Scikit-learn. It includes median 
income, house age, average rooms, population, occupancy, latitude, and longitude. 
Technologies: Python, Jupyter Notebook, Pandas, NumPy, Matplotlib, Seaborn, Scikit
learn, and Joblib. 
3. Methodology 
The dataset was loaded and explored to inspect its features, data types, missing values, 
and statistical distributions. The input features were separated from the target variable, 
HousePrice. 
The dataset was divided into 80% training data and 20% testing data. StandardScaler 
was fitted using the training features and then applied to both training and testing 
features. 
Three regression algorithms were trained: Linear Regression, Ridge Regression, and 
Decision Tree Regression. Their performance was compared using Root Mean Squared 
Error (RMSE), Mean Absolute Error (MAE), and R² score. 
4. Model Performance and Results 
Model 
RMSE 
MAE 
R² Score 
Linear Regression  0.7456 0.5332 0.5758 
Ridge Regression 0.7456 0.5332 0.5758 
Decision Tree 0.7242 0.5223 0.5997 
The model with the lowest test RMSE was selected as the best model. 
Selected model: Decision Tree 
Best model RMSE: 0.7242 
Best model MAE: 0.5223 
Best model R² score: 0.5997 
The actual-versus-predicted plot and residual plot were used to examine prediction 
quality and possible model errors. 
5. Conclusion 
This project demonstrated an end-to-end machine learning workflow for housing price 
prediction. Feature scaling, consistent testing, and comparing multiple algorithms 
helped establish a data-based model-selection process. 
The final model was chosen based on its measured test performance. Further 
improvements could include cross-validation, hyperparameter tuning, and testing 
additional algorithms. 
Deliverables: Jupyter Notebook, model performance comparison table, visualizations, 
and PDF report. 